The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (mtk_wed_device_active(&dev->mmio.wed) &&
    mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(q))
    continue;
```
```c
if (!mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(q))
    mt76_put_page_pool_buf(buf, false);
```
Reason:
The first statement in `mt76_dma_cleanup` skips the entire cleanup (including `page_pool_destroy`) for WED RRO queues when WED is active, causing a memory leak because the page pool is never destroyed. The second statement in `mt76_dma_rx_cleanup` unconditionally skips returning pages to the page pool for all WED RRO queues, even when WED is not active, leading to pages being leaked.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (mtk_wed_device_active(&dev->mmio.wed) &&
    mt76_queue_is_wed_rro(q))
    continue;
if (!mt76_queue_is_wed_rro_rxdmad_c(q) &&
    !mt76_queue_is_wed_rro_ind(q))
    mt76_put_page_pool_buf(buf, false);
```
```c
// In mt76_dma_cleanup, the earlier "if (mtk_wed_device_active(...) && ...) continue;" block is removed.
netif_napi_del(&dev->napi[i]);
mt76_dma_rx_cleanup(dev, q);
page_pool_destroy(q->page_pool);
```
Reason:
In `mt76_dma_rx_cleanup`, the new code correctly skips returning pages to the pool only when WED is active and the queue is a WED RRO queue (since WED manages those pages). Otherwise, it returns pages for RRO queues that are not specific subtypes (rxdmad_c or ind), fixing the leak when WED is inactive. In `mt76_dma_cleanup`, removing the skip ensures that all rx queues (including WED RRO queues) have their NAPI deleted, page pool cleaned up via `mt76_dma_rx_cleanup`, and page pool destroyed, thereby fixing the memory leak of the page pool itself.

